00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.319
Hello world! Shawn Grooms here again with Free Code Camp and in this video I'll be

00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:09.210
explaining what sets are. A set is a
collection of distinct objects called

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.889
elements or members. An element is
essentially anything you want it to be

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:19.199
and for example elements could be
numbers, letters, variables, more sets, or

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:25.140
nothing at all. Sets are usually denoted
by capital letters such as capital A

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:33.149
capital B, C, E, or F and we say that the
set contains elements. For example, we

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:38.850
could say that the set A equals the set
containing the element 5 or we can say

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.680
that 5 is an element of A. Now, I will be
inttoducing a lot of symbols like this

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.989
this just means is an element of and
it'll save us a lot of time in future

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:53.460
videos and I don't have to write out is
an element of each time so that will

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.000
come in handy. So, what does the set
actually look like?

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.260
Well, there are two common types of
  for sets and the one

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.830
I'm interested in this video is roster
notation and it looks like this: it's just

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:09.210
curly braces with elements inside
separated by commas, which is very

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.799
similar to JavaScript - the only
difference being that in math we use

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:22.470
curly braces and in JavaScript we use a
square bracket. So, now let's just walk

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:27.180
through these. So, the set A
equals the set containing the element 5

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.070
- that's how you would read this. The set B
equals the set containing the elements 2, 3,

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:40.200
and 4. The set C equals the set
containing elements d, f, and g. Now, d, f, and g, they're

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.220
not - they don't have to be variables.
They're either variables or they could

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.409
be characters - it just depends on the
context. The set E contains no

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:53.460
elements in this case which has an
impact that you'll see in future videos.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:02:00.270
Finally, the set F equals the set
containing elements A, B and C. In this case,

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:07.409
our elements are sets so we would read
this as F equals the set containing the

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:12.480
set containing the element 5, the set
containing elements 2, 3, and 4,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.930
and the set containing d, f, and g. So, in the next video i'll be going over a

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.280
different form of set notation in
addition to introducing you to some

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.830
common sets and this will set us up

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:31.500
eventually my goal is to bring us up to
being able to define and understand

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.420
algorithms and this is where we have to
begin.

